===Etymology 1===
{{etymon|en|:af|ass<id:buttocks>|hole|tree=1}}
Variant of earlier {{m|enm|arsehole}}, from {{der|en|enm|arshole}}, {{m|enm|arcehoole}}, equivalent to {{af|en|ass|hole}}. Cognate with {{cog|no|rasshøl|t=asshole}}, {{cog|sv|arsle|t=asshole}}. Compare also {{cog|de|Arschloch|t=asshole}}. Attested from the 1370s, replacing earlier {{cog|ang|earsþyrel|t=anus|lit=ass hole}}.
First recorded in Middle English, as ''ers hole'' (Glouc. Cath. Manuscript 19. No. I., dated 1379, cited after OED), ''ars-hole'' (Bodleian Ashmole MS. 1396, dated ca. 1400, ed. Robert Von Fleischhacker as ''Lanfrank's "Science of Cirurgie"'', EETS 102, 1894, cited after OED.) Slang figurative usage dates to the 20th century; it was used of an uninviting place (compare [[shithole]]) in the 1920s, and then of an anti-social or despicable person from at least the 1950s (Harvard Advocate 137, March 1954). It is also used appositionally (as in "You're an asshole moralist", T. Chamales, [http://books.google.com/books?id=js4nAQAAIAAJ&q=%22asshole%22&dq=%22asshole%22&hl=en&ei=9wtmTsynGLTP4QSA1LHHCg&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=1&ved=0CCwQ6AEwAA 1957]).

