bastard:
{{also|Bastard}}
==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|en|bastid||pronunciation spelling}}

===Etymology===
{{etymon|en|:inh|enm:bastard}}
[[File:Wyllelm bastard.jpg|right|thumb|220px|'ƿyllelm bastard' in the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle, 1066]]
From {{inh|en|enm|bastard}}, {{m|enm|bastarde}}, from {{inh|en|ang|bastard|pos=used as an epithet}}, from {{der|en|xno|bastard}}, {{cog|fro|bastart|t=illegitimate child}}, perhaps via {{der|en|la-med|bastardus}}, of {{unk|en|title=obscure}} origin.

Likely from {{der|en|frk|*bāst|t=marriage, relationship}} + {{m+|fro|-ard}}, {{m|fro|-art|pos=pejorative suffix denoting a specific quality or condition}}. Frankish ''*bāst'' derives from a [[w:North Sea Germanic|North Sea Germanic]] variety of {{der|en|gem-pro|*banstuz|t=bond, connection, relationship, marriage with a second woman of lower status}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*bʰendʰ-|t=to tie, bind}} and is related to {{cog|fy|boaste|t=marriage, matrimony}}, {{cog|dum|bast|t=lust, heat}}, and more distantly to {{cog|en|boose|t=cow-stall}}. The term probably originally referred to a child from a [[polygynous]] marriage of [[heathen]] Germanic custom — a practice not sanctioned by the Christian churches.

Alternatively, and less likely, Old French ''bastart'' may have originated from the Old French term {{m|fro|fils de bast|t=[[packsaddle]] son}}, meaning a child conceived on an improvised bed (medieval saddles often doubled as beds while travelling). However chronology makes this difficult, as ''bastard'' is attested in Old French from 1089 (Middle Latin ''bastardus'' as early as 1010), yet Old French ''bast'' (modern {{cog|fr|bât}}), though attested since 1130 with the meaning of "beast of burden", doesn't acquire the specific meaning of "packsaddle" until the 13c., making it too late to have given rise to the terms ''bastard'' and ''bastardus'' with this sense. The French ''Centre National de Ressources Textuelles et Lexicales'' supports the Germanic theory further above as being most likely.<ref>{{R:fr:TLFi|bâtard|etym=1}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|en|UK}}
** {{IPA|en|/ˈbɑːs.təd/|a=RP}}
*** {{audio|en|en-uk-bastard.ogg|a=UK}}
** {{IPA|en|/ˈbæs.tæd/|a=Northumbrian}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈbæs.tɚd/|a=GA}}
** {{audio|en|En-us-bastard.oga|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɑːstə(ɹ)d|æstə(ɹ)d|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|dated}} A person who was born [[out of wedlock]], and hence often considered an [[illegitimate]] descendant.
#: {{syn|en|love child|born in the vestry|illegitimate|Thesaurus:bastard}}
#* {{quote-av|en|year=1965|title=w:The Big Valley
|passage='''Jarrod''': Who are you?<br>'''Heath''': Your father’s '''bastard''' son.}}
# A [[mongrel]] {{gloss|biological [[cross]] between different [[breed]]s, groups or varieties}}.
# {{lb|en|[[vulgar]],|_||_|offensive|or|pejorative|usually|referring specifically to a man}} A [[contemptible]], [[inconsiderate]], overly or [[arrogantly]] [[rude]] or [[spiteful]] person.
#: {{syn|en|son of a bitch|arsehole|asshole|Thesaurus:git|Thesaurus:jerk}}
#: {{ux|en|Some '''bastard''' stole my car while I was helping an injured person.}}
#: {{ux|en|You sick '''bastard!'''}}
#: {{ux|en|Don't be such a '''bastard''' already!}}
#: {{ux|en|I assume that '''bastard''' won't be seen again.}}
#* '''1997''', ''{{w|South Park}}'' television program:
#*: “Oh my God, they killed Kenny!” “You '''bastards'''!”
# {{lb|en|vulgar|endearing|or|humorous}} A man, a fellow, a male friend.
#: {{co|en|lucky '''bastard'''}}
#: {{co|en|funny '''bastard'''}}
#: {{ux|en|Get over here, you old '''bastard'''!}}
# {{lb|en|vulgar|often preceded by ‘poor’}} A suffering person deemed deserving of [[compassion]].
#: {{ux|en|Poor '''bastard''', I feel so sorry for him.}}
#: {{ux|en|These poor '''bastards''' started out life probably in bad or broken homes.}}
# {{lb|en|informal}} A child who does not know their [[father]].
# {{lb|en|informal}} Something extremely difficult or unpleasant to deal with.
#: {{ux|en|Life can be a real '''bastard'''.}}
# A variation that is not [[genuine]]; something irregular or inferior or of dubious origin, [[fake]] or [[counterfeit]].
#: {{ux|en|The architecture was a kind of '''bastard''', suggesting Gothic but not being true Gothic.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1622|author=w:Francis Bacon|title=Bacon's History of the Reign of King Henry VII|page=62|publisher=Cambridge University Press|year_published=1902
|passage=There were also made good and politic laws that parliament, against usury, which is the '''bastard''' use of money...}}
