##: {{coi|en|archery '''butt'''|archery '''target'''}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 5|I|II|line=186|text=To which is fixed, as an aim or '''butt'''{{...}}}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1786|author=Francis Grose|title=A Treatise on Ancient Armour and Weapons|page=37
|passage=The inhabitants of all cities and towns were ordered to make '''butts''', and to keep them in repair, under a penalty of twenty shillings per month, and to exercise themselves in shooting at them on holidays.}}
##* {{RQ:Dryden Georgics|2|passage=The groom his fellow groom at '''butts''' defies, / And bends his bow, and levels with his eyes.}}
## {{senseid|en|butt of joke}} {{q|usually as "butt of (a) joke"}} A person at whom [[ridicule]], [[jest]], or [[contempt]] is directed.
##: {{syn|en|laughing stock}}
##: {{ux|en|He's usually the '''butt''' of their jokes.}}
