===Etymology===
{{ety|en|:bor|la-new:clītoris|tree=1}}
From post-classical {{der|en|la-new|clītoris}} (16th century), from {{der|en|grc-koi|κλειτορίς}}, probably from {{der|en|grc|κλείω||to sheathe, shut}}, in reference to it being covered by the [[labia minora]].

The related noun form {{m|grc|κλείς}} has a second meaning of "a key, a latch or hook (to close a door)." Wooden pegs were the original keys; a connection also revealed in Latin {{m|la|clavis||nail}} and {{m|la|claudere||to shut}} (see [[close#Verb|close]]). Some medical sources give a supposed Greek verb {{m|grc|κλειτοριάζω||[[touch]] or [[titillate]] [[lasciviously]], [[tickle]]}} literally "to be inclined (toward pleasure)" (compare {{cog|de|Kitzler||clitoris|lit=tickler}}, related to Greek {{m|grc|κλειτύς||shut, closed}}, a variant of {{m|grc|κλιτύς||hillside}}, related to {{m|grc|κλίνω||to slope}}, from the same root as {{m|grc|κλῖμαξ||ladder}}. But many sources take {{m|grc|κλειτορίς}} literally as Ancient Greek "little hill".

