====Usage notes====
* The usage to refer to the British meatball delicacy ({{sno|en|Q510387}}) is not widely known outside the United Kingdom and Ireland. Due to the prevalence of the usage as a homophobic slur ({{sno|en|gay man}}) in other regions, it is likely to be misconstrued as hate speech by those unaware of it.<ref>{{cite-web|author=Bradley Jolly|title=Meat lover, 55, banned from Facebook after he praised butcher's faggots|url=https://www.mirror.co.uk/news/uk-news/meat-lover-55-banned-facebook-21329708|work=Mirror Online|date=21 January 2020|accessdate=16 June 2023|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260616132134/https://www.mirror.co.uk/news/uk-news/meat-lover-55-banned-facebook-21329708|archivedate=16 June 2026}}</ref><ref>{{RQ:Telegraph|author=Miranda Prynne|title=Man banned from Facebook for liking faggots|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/technology/facebook/10419598/Man-banned-from-Facebook-for-liking-faggots.html|date=1 November 2013|accessdate=13 June 2023|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20251008043854/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/technology/facebook/10419598/Man-banned-from-Facebook-for-liking-faggots.html|archivedate=8 October 2025|ascite=1}}</ref><ref>{{cite-web|author=Martin Fricker|title=History group threatened with Facebook ban for discussing local delicacy - faggots|url=https://www.mirror.co.uk/news/uk-news/history-group-threatened-facebook-ban-23519919|work=Mirror Online|date=17 February 2021|accessdate=16 June 2023|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260616132134/https://www.mirror.co.uk/news/uk-news/history-group-threatened-facebook-ban-23519919|archivedate=16 June 2026}}</ref><ref>{{RQ:Independent|author=Chiara Giordano|title=Faggots and peas advert banned by Google for ‘inappropriate and offensive content’|url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/uk/home-news/faggots-peas-advert-banned-google-fanny-s-cafe-newport-wales-a9099061.html|date=10 September 2019|accessdate=16 June 2023|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20230616044823/https://www.independent.co.uk/news/uk/home-news/faggots-peas-advert-banned-google-fanny-s-cafe-newport-wales-a9099061.html|archivedate=16 June 2023|ascite=1}}</ref> In contexts where the word can be interpreted as an allusion to homosexuals, this sense can be considered offensive even in the United Kingdom and Ireland, despite the homophobic slur not being in widespread use there.<ref>{{RQ:Telegraph|title=Ofcom bans 'derogatory' faggot advert|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/1466223/Ofcom-bans-derogatory-faggot-advert.html|date=5 July 2004|accessdate=6 December 2023|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20251202025738/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/1466223/Ofcom-bans-derogatory-faggot-advert.html|archivedate=2 December 2025|ascite=1}}</ref>
* In some dictionaries, such as [[Wiktionary:Merriam-Webster|Merriam-Webster]], the term, in its archaic sense, was derogatory towards women and children, "a disagreeble child or woman".<ref>{{R:Merriam-Webster|faggot}}</ref>
* As a slur for a gay man, it is usually very derogatory when used by straight people. It is also used by some gay and bisexual men to refer to themselves positively; see {{w|reclaimed word}} and {{w|reappropriation}}.

