===Etymology===
{{etymon|en|id=copulate|:inh|enm:*fukken<id:copulate>|tree=1}}
From {{inh|en|enm|*fukken}},<ref>Not directly attested, but found in names, and in code hybridized with Latin in {{circa|1475}}, ''{{w|Flen flyys}}'' (Hrl 3362), page 91: Fratres Carmeli navigant in a bothe apud Eli, / Non sunt in cœli, quia gxddbov xxkxzt pg ifmk [=fuccant uuiuys of Heli]. / Omnes drencherunt, quia sterisman non habuerunt, / Fratres cum knyvys goth about and txxkxzv nfookt xxzxkt [=suuiuyt mennis uuyuis].</ref> probably of {{der|en|gem}} origin: either from {{der|en|ang|*fuccian}} or {{der|en|non|*fukka}}, both from {{der|en|gem-pro|*fukkōną|id=strike}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*pewǵ-|t=to strike, punch, stab}}. Compare {{m|en|windfucker}} and its debated etymology.

Possibly attested in a 772 {{CE}} charter that mentions a place called {{m|ang||Fuccerham}}, which may mean "{{m|ang|hām|t=home}} of the fucker" or "{{m|ang|hamm|t=pasture}} of the fucker"; a ''John le Fucker'' in a record from 1278 may just be a variant of {{m|en|Fulcher}}; compare {{m|en||Fucher}}, {{m|en||Foker}}, etc.<ref name="Liberman">{{cite-book|author=Anatoly Liberman|authorlink=Anatoly Liberman|entry=FUCK|title=An Analytic Dictionary of the English Etymology: An Introduction|url=https://issuu.com/ifolderlinks/docs/an-analytic-dictionary-of-english-e|location=Minneapolis, Minn.|publisher={{w|University of Minnesota Press}}|year=2008|pages=78-87|isbn=978-0-8166-5272-3}}</ref> The earliest unambiguous use of the word in a clearly sexual context, in any stage of English, appears to be in court documents from [[Cheshire]], England, which mention a man called ''Roger Fuckebythenavele'' (possibly [[tongue-in-cheek]] or directly suggestive of a depraved sexual act) on 8 December 1310.<ref>[http://www.medievalists.net/2015/09/10/the-earliest-use-of-the-f-word-discovered/ The earliest use of the F-word discovered], Medievalists.net, 2015-09-10</ref><ref>Loulla-Mae Eleftheriou-Smith, "[http://www.independent.co.uk/news/uk/home-news/historian-understood-to-have-found-first-use-of-word-f-in-1310-english-court-case-concerning-roger-fuckebythenavele-10498958.html Historian understood to have found first use of word f*** in 1310 English court case]", ''The Independent'', 13 September 2015</ref> It was first listed in a dictionary in 1598.<ref>[http://www.slate.com/id/2227971/ Can a Woman "Prong" a Man? Why it’s so hard to put sex in the dictionary], by Jesse Sheidlower, 2009-10-01, {{w|Slate.com}}</ref> {{cog|sco|fuk}} or {{m|sco|fuck}} is attested slightly earlier, probably reinforcing the Northern Germanic/Scandinavian origin theory. From 1500 onward, the word has been in continual use, superseding {{m|en|jape}} and {{m|en|sard}} and largely displacing {{m|en|swive}}.<ref name="Liberman"/> See {{m|en|windfucker}} and {{m|en|fuckwind}} for more information.

A range of {{glossary|folk-etymological}} {{glossary|backronyms}}, such as ''[[fornication]] under consent of the king'' and ''for unlawful [[carnal knowledge]]'', are all demonstrably false.

