===Etymology===
{{etymon|en|id=Jewish|:der|yi:קײַקל<id:circle><unc>|tree=1}}
Originally used by German-American Jews as a slur for Eastern European Jews immigrating to the United States.<ref>{{cite-web|title=Etymology of kike|titleurl=https://www.etymonline.com/word/kike|year=2026|author=Harper Doughlas|work=Online Etymology Dictionary|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260406200941/https://www.etymonline.com/word/kike|archivedate=6 April 2026}}</ref>

The most dominant theory is from {{der|en|yi|קײַקל||circle}}. In the early 20th century, non-English-speaking Jews that immigrated to the United States would sign papers with a circle as opposed to a more common X, in order to avoid using a symbol reminiscent of a Christian Cross.<ref>{{cite-book |last=Rosten |first=Leo |authorlink=Leo_Rosten |title=The Joys of Yiddish |location=New York |publisher=Pocket Books |year=1968}} Cited in {{cite-web|title=kike|archivedate=2 June 2008|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20080602102925/http://kpearson.faculty.tcnj.edu/Dictionary/kike.htm|titleurl=http://kpearson.faculty.tcnj.edu/Dictionary/kike.htm|others=(A rare usage is "kyke".)|year=2003|author=Kim Pearson|work=kpearson.faculty.tcnj.edu|accessdate=3 June 2016}}
</ref> If so, a {{doublet|en|chakra|chakram|charkha|chukker|cycle|cyclus|wheel|nocap=1}}.

Other theories also exist, in particular a contraction from the documented phrase “Ikey-Kikey”, an American-origin reduplication of {{m|en|ikey}}, which in turn is a British-English pejorative for Jews after the prevalence of the name {{m|en|Isaac}}.<ref>
{{cite-web|title=kike|archivedate=2 June 2008|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20080602102925/http://kpearson.faculty.tcnj.edu/Dictionary/kike.htm|titleurl=http://kpearson.faculty.tcnj.edu/Dictionary/kike.htm|others=(A rare usage is "kyke".)|year=2003|author=Kim Pearson|work=kpearson.faculty.tcnj.edu|accessdate=3 June 2016}}</ref>

Alternatively, from {{der|en|sco|keek|t=to {{m|en|peek}} or {{m|en|peep}} surreptitiously}}, used in the New York City clothing industry to refer to the practice of sending spies to work in competing garment factories to copy designs which would then be reproduced and sold at lower cost. Eastern European Jews played a major role in the clothing industry and were perceived to frequently participate in underhanded business tactics. Originally mostly used by older, established German Jews to refer to the Eastern European newcomers, eventually it became generalized to all Jews.<ref>{{cite-web
|url=https://mischrev.substack.com/p/how-jews-became-kikes|entry= How Jews Became Kikes|author=Wittmayer|date=14 January 2024|work=Mischling Revolution}}
</ref>

