====Usage notes====
*The usage to mean ''angry'' was originally confined to the United States, but has spread to other regions including Australia and Canada (and even the United Kingdom, though the term {{m|en|pissed off}} is more common in this context there). Outside the [[United States]], '''''pissed''''' can mean either ''drunk'' or ''angry''; the term {{m|en|pissed off}} is commonly used to unambiguously give the meaning ''angry'', while {{m|en|pissed drunk}} unambiguously gives the meaning of ''drunk'' (unless '''''pissed''''' is being used as an adjective while ''drunk'' is used as a noun, in which case it becomes ambiguous again). In the [[United Kingdom]], [[Ireland]] and [[Commonwealth of Nations]], the terms {{m|en|pissed as a newt}} and {{m|en|pissed as a fart}} can be used to unambiguously give the meaning ''drunk'', though these two terms are stronger than '''''pissed''''' on its own.
*While using '''''pissed''''' on its own to mean ''drunk'' is normally chiefly used in countries where Commonwealth English is spoken, the set phrase {{m|en|pissed drunk}} is also used in the United States, where '''''pissed''''' otherwise only has the meaning of being angry.

