Uncertain. The ''OED'', which connects the senses "blow" and "shower" only tentatively, suggests that at least the former might be onomatopoeia.<ref name="OEDscat">{{R:OED}}</ref> Dialectally, the word can also refer to "a spell" of any kind of weather, e.g. "a scat of fine weather", "a scat of frost".<ref name="OEDscat"/>

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|uncommon}} A [[blow]]; a hit, an impact.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1895|author=Samuel Rutherford Crockett|title=Bog-myrtle and Peat: Tales Chiefly of Galloway Gathered from the Years 1889 to 1895|publisher=London, Bliss, Sands and Foster|page=294
|text=... a shot rang out, followed immediately by the "'''scat'''" of a bullet against the rock behind which he lay concealed. A tramp of heavy Galloway brogans was heard, and a half-hearted kicking about among the heather bushes, and at last{{nb...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1901|author=Ethel Lilian Voynich|title=Jack Raymond|page=173|text=... the soft and pitying eyes seemed to shame him, "like a '''scat''' in the face," he said to himself. But who was he that he should care for any blow across the cheek now, if it was not hard enough to hurt?}}
# {{lb|en|UK|dialect}} A brisk [[shower]] of [[rain]], driven by the wind.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1759|author=Andrew Brice|title=The Grand Gazetteer, or Topographic Dictionary|page=677|text=Low black Clouds on it being ſoppoſed to prognoſticate Rain in the Places beneath it, it has been a ſtanding old Saw, ''When Haldon hath a Hat, Kenton beware a '''Skat'''.''|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=JHZaAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA677}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1897|author=Eden Phillpotts|title=Lying Prophets: A Novel|page=187
|text=... a scat of heavy rain on a squall of wind shut out the harbour for a time. Mrs. Tregenza waited until Joan had disappeared, then went back to her kitchen, closed the door, sat in Grey Michael's great chair by the hearth,{{nb...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=2019-12-09|origyear=????|author=E. Phillips Oppenheim|title=Nobody's Man: A Thrilling Tale of Espionage and Betrayal|publisher=Good Press
|text="I am never away." The tail end of a '''scat''' of rain beat on their faces. From the hollow on their left, the wind came booming up.}}

