===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*(s)kewd-}}
Pseudo-archaic alteration of {{m|en|shoot}}, perhaps with reference to [[Old Norse]] {{m|non|skjóta}}; compare {{cog|sco|skite||to dart, to shoot}}.<ref>{{cite-web|title=skeet|titleurl=http://dictionary.reference.com/browse/skeet |work=Dictionary.com|accessdate=2023-05-16|text=First recorded in 1925–30; supposedly as the result of a contest to choose a name for the sport (the winner claimed that the word was “a very old form” of shoot)|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20151115121855/http://dictionary.reference.com:80/browse/skeet|archivedate=15 November 2015}}</ref> The name for a form of trapshooting is attested from the 1920s,<ref name="etymonline">{{R:Etymonline|skeet|2023-05-16}}</ref> see quotations below. Senses related to ejaculation of semen likely derive from this, but compare also {{m|en|squirt}},<ref>{{R:GDoS|id=zxe6hqq|entry=skeet|pos=v<sup>1</sup>|accessdate=2023-05-16}}</ref> {{m|en|skite}}, or {{m|en|scoot}}.<ref>{{R:OED Online|skeet|v|180675|March 2023}}</ref> The word ''skeet'' is attested in reference to working class persons in US English from the 19th century,<ref name="etymonline" /> or the Newfoundland and Labrador regionalism may derive from other terms such as ''skite'' or {{m|en|skeeter}}; see quotation below.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|uncountable}} A [[form]] of [[trapshooting]] using clay targets to [[simulate]] birds in [[flight]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1929|title=Skeet|journal=West Virginia Wildlife|page=22|text=THE ARTICLE on the sport of '''Skeet''' that appeared in the June issue of WILD LIFE described the layout of the '''Skeet''' field, installation of the traps, and the rules and regulations for '''Skeet''' shooting.|volume=7}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1932|author=Charles Askins|title=Notes on skeet|journal=Outdoor Life|page=40|text=The longer I shoot '''skeet''' the more convinced I am that it is the greatest game ever devised for the users of shotguns. '''Skeet''' has brought home to shooters the need of properly fitting guns and the benefit of straighter stocks}}
#* {{RQ:SciAm|year=1940|title=In the Spring—Skeet|page=363|text=To Mrs. Gertrude Hurlbutt, Montana rancher’s wife, went a $100 prize in 1926 from ''National Sportsman'' and ''Hunting and Fishing'' magazines for christening their newly sponsored shotgun sport “'''skeet'''” – Scandinavian derivation, meaning “to shoot.”|brackets=on}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2007|author=King Heiple|title=Mastering Skeet|page=163|text='''Skeet''' started informally in 1920 as off-season practice for bird hunting, so it needed few rules. It became more formally organized in 1926 when the National '''Skeet''' Shooting Association (NSSA) was formed.|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=M0lOsWSNDgoC&pg=PA163}}
# {{lb|en|countable|poker}} A hand consisting of a 9, a 5, a 2, and two other cards lower than 9.
# {{lb|en|uncountable|slang|AAVE}} The [[ejaculation]] of [[semen]].
# {{lb|en|countable|Newfoundland|Labrador|slang}} A young working-class person who may be [[loud]], [[disruptive]] and poorly [[educated]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2010|author=Sandra Clarke|title=Newfoundland and Labrador English|url=https://www.google.co.uk/books/edition/Newfoundland_and_Labrador_English/WO6qBgAAQBAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=chavvy+skeet&pg=PA151&printsec=frontcover|page=151|passage=A small part at least of the language associated with younger speakers originates in local words that appear to have undergone local meaning change. A good example is '''''skeet'''''. This term may be related to ''skite'' which in neighbouring Prince Edward Island can mean a 'young scoundrel' (Pratt 1988), or to the American terms ''skeester''/''skeeter'', definied by the ''Dictionary of American Regional English'' (Cassidy and Hall 1985) as 'rascal, rogue'. If older NLE speakers know this word at all, they would probably use it in this sense. Among younger speakers, however, it has much the same meaning as such British slang terms as ''chav'', ''charver'' or ''scally'', or even the North American ''white trash''.|brackets=on}}

