swastika:
{{also|Swastika|卐|卍}}
==English==
[[File:HinduSwastika.svg|right|thumb|Hindu swastika]]
[[File:National Socialist swastika.svg|right|thumb|Nazi swastika]]

===Etymology===
{{lbor|en|sa|स्वस्तिक}}, from {{m|sa|सु-||good, well}} + {{m|sa|अस्ति}}, a verbal abstract of the root of the verb "to be", {{m|sa|स्वस्ति}} thus meaning "well-being" — and the diminutive suffix {{m|sa|क}}; hence "little thing associated with well-being", corresponding roughly to "lucky charm". First attestation in English in 1871, a Sanskritism that replaced the Grecian term {{m|en|gammadion}}. From 1932 onwards it often referred specifically to the version used by the [[NSDAP|National Socialist German Workers’ Party]] (also called the  "hooked cross", or {{ncog|de|Hakenkreuz}}).

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈswɒstɪkə/|/ˈswɒstəkə/|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈswɑstɪkə/|/ˈswɑstəkə/|a=GA}}
** {{audio|en|en-us-swastika.ogg|a=US}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈswɔstɘkɘ/|a=NZ}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|countable}} A [[cross]] with arms of equal length all bent halfway along at a 90° angle to the right or to the left, used as a religious symbol by various ancient and modern civilizations, but now mainly seen and used in the West (with arms angled to the right) as a symbol of [[Nazism]] and [[fascism]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1888|author=w:Rudyard Kipling|chapter=s:The Sending of Dana Da|title=In Black and White|publisher=Folio Society|year_published=2005|pages=423–4|passage=This was signed by Dana Da, who added pentacles and pentagrams, and a ''crux ansata'', and half-a-dozen '''''swastikas''''', and a Triple Tau to his name, just to show that he was all he laid claim to be.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2009|author=w:Diarmaid MacCulloch|title=A History of Christianity|publisher=Penguin|year_published=2010|page=270|passage=It is clear from archaeological finds that they enjoyed wearing Christian crosses, though they might enliven these with such symbols as the Indian '''swastika''' which Buddhists had brought them.}}
#* {{RQ:en:CNN|author=Eric Bradner and Adrienne Shih|title=Local GOP office in North Carolina firebombed|url=https://www.cnn.com/2016/10/16/politics/north-carolina-gop-office-vandalized/index.html|date=2016-10-16|passage=A GOP office in Hillsborough, North Carolina, was firebombed over the weekend, with a '''swastika''' and the words “Nazi Republicans leave town or else” spray painted on an adjacent building, according to local officials.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20261008184326/https://www.cnn.com/2016/10/16/politics/north-carolina-gop-office-vandalized/index.html|archivedate=8 October 2026}}
#* {{RQ:en:CNN|author=Sara Smart and Elizabeth Wolfe|title=Swastikas found drawn on Connecticut high school campus for second time since Israel-Hamas war ignited, district says|url=https://www.cnn.com/2023/10/30/us/connecticut-stamford-high-school-swastika-vandalism/index.html|date=2023-10-30|passage=Authorities in Connecticut are investigating after '''swastikas''' were found drawn on a high school campus in Stamford for the second time since Hamas launched a surprise attack on Israel earlier this month, the school district said.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260301161841/https://www.cnn.com/2023/10/30/us/connecticut-stamford-high-school-swastika-vandalism/index.html|archivedate=1 March 2026}}
# {{lb|en|fascism|history|metonym|uncountable}} [[Nazi]] [[rule]].
#* {{quote-av|en|year=1989|actor=Alison Doody|role=Dr. Elsa Schneider|title=Indiana Jones and the Last Crusade|text="Is that what you think of me? I believe in the Grail, not the '''Swastika'''!"}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Rudolph Herzog|title=Dead Funny: Humor in Hitler's Germany|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=aTIsAPQO1L0C|location=Brooklyn|publisher=Melville House|year=2011|page=36|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=aTIsAPQO1L0C&pg=PT36|tlr=Jefferson Chase|ISBN=1935554301|text=“I can’t imagine anyone believes in Communist culprits instead of a contract job commissioned on behalf of the '''swastika'''.”}}

